This note states no numbered theorem: a nonempty set \(\Delta\) of tokens determines neighbourhoods as subsets of \(\Delta\), the family \(\mathcal{D}\) of allowed neighbourhoods is a neighbourhood system, a finite sequence drawn from \(\mathcal{D}\) is consistent when one \(Z \in \mathcal{D}\) is contained in every member (for a pair, \(Z \subseteq X\) and \(Z \subseteq Y\)), and a consistent intersection is again a neighbourhood, so that the system can carry partial and total elements as a domain; the rest of the note is a list of links from Definition 1.1 through Exercise 1.27 and does not state those items. `NeighborhoodSystem` is a membership predicate together with a nonempty master, membership of that master, containment of every neighbourhood in the master, and `inter_mem`, which puts \(X \cap Y\) in \(\mathcal{D}\) whenever \(X, Y, Z \in \mathcal{D}\) and \(Z \subseteq X \cap Y\). `interUpTo`, `interUpTo_zero`, `interUpTo_succ`, and `interUpTo_antitone` cut a sequence down to a finite prefix, with the empty intersection equal to the master, and `Consistent` holds when some neighbourhood sits inside that prefix intersection; `Basic` proves that a finite sequence of neighbourhoods is consistent exactly when the prefix intersection itself lies in \(\mathcal{D}\). `Element` defines an element as a filter of neighbourhoods containing the master, closed under binary intersection, and upward closed in \(\mathcal{D}\); `element_le`, `element_le_refl`, `element_le_trans`, `element_le_antisymm`, `ext`, and `BasicInst22` make filter inclusion a partial order; `principal` and `mem_principal` are the principal filter of neighbourhoods containing a given neighbourhood; `bot`, `mem_bot`, `bot_le`, and `BasicInst34` give the least element as the principal filter of the master, whose only member is the master; `IsTotal` says an element is total when it is maximal; `DomainIso`, `Isomorphic`, and `Basic/notation40` take isomorphism of systems to be an order isomorphism of their element posets; `IsPositive` strengthens intersection closure to the biconditional that \(X \cap Y\) is a neighbourhood exactly when it is nonempty, and `ofPositive` rebuilds a system from that law, while `NestedOrDisjoint` and `ofNestedOrDisjoint` rebuild a system from a family that contains its master and is pairwise nested or disjoint; `SeqEquiv` and `limitFamily` match sequences in which each term contains some term of the other with the neighbourhoods lying above some term. Example 1.B, assembled by `Str`, `cone`, `cone_nil`, `cone_trichotomy`, `cone_subset_cone`, `cone_injective`, `memB`, `nestedOrDisjoint`, `B`, `B_master`, and `B_mem`, takes finite binary strings as tokens and cones of prefixes as neighbourhoods, with master the set of all strings, equal to the cone of the empty string; `prepend`, `prepend_cone`, `prepend_univ`, `sigmaBot`, `sigmaElt`, `sigmaBot_le_iff`, and `sigmaElt_append` send a string to the principal filter of its cone and let concatenation act by successive application, with \(s\) below \(t\) in the finite-element order exactly when \(s\) is a prefix of \(t\). Exercise 1.23’s `state`, `cond`, `Y`, `acc`, `acc_mem`, `acc_eq_interUpTo`, `mem_interUpTo_Y`, and `greedyElement` walk an enumeration of neighbourhoods, keep the next set when its intersection with the running accumulator is a neighbourhood, and form the filter of neighbourhoods above some finite intersection of the sets that were kept. The prose takes \(\Delta\) itself to be the set of tokens, whereas `NeighborhoodSystem` quantifies neighbourhoods over an ambient type and requires only that the master subset be nonempty and contain every neighbourhood. The filter definition of an element, the approximation order, \(\bot = \{\Delta\}\), totality as maximality, positivity, the binary cone system, and the greedy filter are developments of the linked Definition 1.6, Definition 1.8, Definition 1.9, Exercise 1.19, Example 1.B, and Exercise 1.23; their statements do not appear in this prose, and their sidecars use the same consistency witness, a neighbourhood contained in an intersection, that the prose adopts. **Theorem:** partial — `inter_mem` and `Consistent` are the intersection rule and the finite consistency relation spelled in the prose, while the master package is the linked Definition 1.1 and the remaining stems assert later definitions, an example, and an exercise that this note does not state. **Proof:** no — the note gives no proof; the Lean arguments discharge those later constructions.
